<p>Manton <a href="https://www.manton.org/2026/10/21/the-bookmarks-links-tab-in.html">writes</a> of Micro.blog&rsquo;s Bookmarks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Bookmarks → Links tab in Micro.blog Premium is practically unknown, but so useful. It archives a copy of everything you link to! HTML, CSS, everything.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I didn&rsquo;t know that&rsquo;s what it did. The button for Links was there, but I never looked into it. All the links I ever linked to. Indexed, searchable, and apparently also <em>archived</em> as if I bookmarked them myself. I can see how this can be useful.</p>
<p>I&rsquo;ve <a href="https://taonaw.com/2025/08/01/thinking-about-organizing-my-rss.html">discussed Micro.blog&rsquo;s Bookmarks</a>, which is a read-it-later feature, <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/06/29/my-inspiration-checklist-for-writing.html">before</a>. It comes with similar features you&rsquo;d expect from such a service, like tagging. For me, this is a simple but essential part of my workflow. In general, I save two kinds of texts: those that I want to read later (and potentially write about), and those I use as a quick syncing option between my phones.</p>
<p>As I was using the bathroom the other day, reading through some of those (maybe this should be another category?), I did some house cleaning and removed a bunch of these. The reason I bother is mostly to keep it fresh and inviting. As any person who ever used a to-do list would tell you, there&rsquo;s nothing that drains your motivation as quickly as an endless to-do list — which these saved articles basically are. I was also wondering if there&rsquo;s a better way to sort those somehow, which raised the question of how other people do it.</p>
<p>Tagging is an option, and I tried tagging at some point, but it turned out to be just noise in the end since everything I save is potentially a post waiting to be written (the other option, for syncing, means the articles get deleted as soon as I have them on my other device). Then I thought maybe I should just create blog drafts for whatever I want to write, but that would introduce friction with the app that I don&rsquo;t want to have. It would also be useful to have an option to save these to the Internet Archive, but I can see why this would be an issue quickly.</p>
<p>So the question remains. How do other people use read-it-later services/apps, if they still do?</p>
